it's no secret that super mario 64  revolutionized the video game industry  and fundamentally changed the way we  play games with the recent release of  super mario 3d all-stars many gamers  could be playing for the first time  others may still be on the fence asking  does super mario 64  still hold up in 2020 that's our topic  for today's video  people of the internet retrorecontour  here if we're meeting for the first time  i'm just a dad who loves games and the  stories they  tell we post videos twice a week here on  youtube and today we're talking all  about super mario 64 and how well it  holds up here in the year 2020.  should you play it if you have never  experienced it before i hope to answer  that question  and maybe a few more here in today's  video  many gamers my age are re-living their  childhood right now as three  classic mario games have taken the  spotlight once again thanks to the  recent release of super mario 3d  all-stars  and we have the younger gamers out there  like my son who were checking out 64  sunshine and galaxy for the very first  time there's no denying that for its  time super mario 64 was an absolute  masterpiece  when it released in 1996 alongside  nintendo's new console  the nintendo 64 gamers were absolutely  blown away to see the italian plumber in  3d for the first time  trust me i was there if you're a younger  gamer out there or maybe an older gamer  who didn't play games as a kid  just try and put yourselves in the mind  of a gamer back in 1996.  for starters a third-person 3d  perspective is something we've never  experienced before most games on home  consoles were side scrollers like the  original super mario brothers  of course you also had games with a top  down view like the original legend of  zelda  over on pc you had shooters like  wolfenstein 3d and doom experimenting  with first person perspectives  so going back and playing super mario 64  now  here in 2020 is a fascinating experience  i recently started playing through it  again over on my twitch stream which  by the way i'd love to have you guys  stop by and check out sometime if you're  interested  right now we stream on monday and  wednesday evenings  i'll throw a link down in the  description below but as for mario 64 i  was immediately struck by  how many of the game mechanics are still  being used today  in numerous third-person games i mean  there's actually a giant  see-saw-like structure early in the game  that immediately reminded me of this  summer smash hit fall guys  even something as simple as the ability  to walk directly up to a sign or an npc  and interact with him in 3d was  something we had never experienced  before and now it's just a given in a  third person game something you just  don't even think about  in the days of the nes and snes nintendo  was known for their incredible level  design  that certainly carried over to mario 64  and still holds up  today peach's castle acts as a hub world  while paintings throughout the castle  transport you to world with completely  different aesthetics  from snow to underwater to lava all the  classics  they're there not to mention all the  secrets you can discover in  and around peach's castle yet again it's  just  incredible how many of the ideas here  still carry over into our games today  at this point you may be thinking oh  this sounds great you should know though  there are some frustrations with mario  64 and some clear  examples where it shows its age the most  obvious one would be the camera  nintendo certainly had the right idea  for controlling the character with the  analog stick which is still the primary  form of movement for console gamers to  this day  what they weren't quite able to figure  out back then was the camera  you see the n64 controller had only one  analog stick instead of a second stick  the controller had a series of four  directional buttons on the right side of  the controller these were originally  intended to be used for camera control  which is what mario 64 did  now even though both the joy cons and  the switch pro controller have two  analog sticks the right analog stick  still behaves in a way that is  essentially just four buttons  left right up and down you don't have  full 360 degree control  over the camera here's a little tip if  you're having trouble with the camera  try not  moving the camera so much like you would  in a modern third-person game with full  camera control  instead let it do its thing most of the  time and simply adjust  how you're moving mario in time you'll  get more comfortable having to move  mario in directions other than just  straight ahead now if you're playing  super mario 64 for the first time thanks  to super mario 3d all-stars go ahead and  leave me a comment down below let me  know what you guys think so far  it's not only the camera though that's a  little wonky even moving mario can feel  strange at first  although an analog stick was certainly  the right call to control mario it's not  as responsive as i remember  it could be because mario odyssey is  fresh in my mind  in fact if you've played odyssey  recently you'll instantly notice the  stark difference here  odyssey's movement controls are precise  and sharp whereas 64's controls feel a  little loose  almost delayed slightly and nowhere near  as sharp  truth be told i expected the control  issues and i have to admit it's  certainly something you get more  comfortable with as you play  the issue that surprised me the most  though the difficulty  super mario 64 is actually a lot harder  than i remember  at least if you're going for all the  stars i'm sure a quick play through  would be a lot easier but my son has had  a difficult time getting into 64 and  while he's only six  he's practically an expert at super  mario odyssey seriously he has over 700  moons  all by himself don't get me wrong some  stars are absurdly easy but others will  have you banging your head against the  wall when you can't get the controls  just right  there's one particular star early in the  game where you have to use the wall jump  for the first time  and i don't know man there's just  something weird about the responsiveness  of the wall jump in this game it doesn't  always work the way i think it should in  my head  it definitely doesn't work the way that  modern wall jumps do in current mario  games  there's also this weird issue where you  can't seem to back out of a level  but pro tip just stop moving mario  and then pause the game seriously it's  so strange but if you're moving mario  and pause the game you won't be able to  leave the level  if you stop moving though and then pause  you'll have the option to exit  so where do i come down on our question  that we open the video with does super  mario  64 still hold up in 2020.  absolutely positively yes  are there some frustrations that you'll  have to overlook in your quest to beat  the game or go after all 120 stars  absolutely positively yes but just from  a simple standpoint of being a fan of  video games you owe it to yourself to  play one of the defining games in the  history of the medium  trust me guys you will be amazed as you  notice numerous little details and  gameplay design choices that still  to this day impact our games in 2020 and  i'm sure beyond  playing super mario 64 also gives me a  completely new  appreciation for super mario odyssey  which is an absolutely  brilliant evolution it still has all the  dna of mario 64 everywhere you look  honestly looking back at all the 3d  mario games you can see their steady  improvement with each generation but all  the while maintaining the spirit of the  original  that started it all now if you guys want  to get your hands on super mario 3d  all-stars check my link  in the description below and remember  nintendo is for some inexplicable reason  making this game only available for a  limited time  it's annoying it's frustrating let  nintendo know about it if you don't want  to buy the game because of it  don't now if you can't get a physical  copy at the link below you can always go  for a digital version straight from  nintendo as well  once again i have to thank you guys for  all the support you've been showing the  channel lately if you're new here and  you're not yet subscribed please  consider hitting that subscribe button  and don't forget to ring the bell so you  won't miss any of our new videos  a thumbs up always helps as well and as  always thank you so much for watching  today's video  i'll talk to you again soon
 

